xfile_parse.c / xfile_mszip.c — vendored from Wine's d3dxof module
(dlls/d3dxof/parsing.c and dlls/d3dxof/mszip.c), commit tracking
wine-mirror/wine master as of 2026-09-13.

License: LGPL 2.1 (Copyright 2004-2008 Christian Costa), carried in each
file's header comment - do not strip it.

What was kept: the tokenizer/parser (text and binary .x, both plain and
MSZIP-compressed) and the MSZIP decompressor. Both are self-contained
logic with no Windows API calls beyond a handful of primitive typedefs
and two heap calls.

What was NOT ported: dlls/d3dxof/d3dxof.c and main.c, the COM
(IDirectXFile*/IUnknown/HRESULT) wrapper Wine built around the same
parser. This engine's LoaderX (engine/src/world/LoaderX.cpp) calls the
parser directly and walks the resulting xobject tree itself - there is
no COM layer here for it to implement.

Changes from upstream, all mechanical:
 - Windows typedefs (BOOL, BYTE, WORD, DWORD, LPBYTE, LPVOID, LPCSTR,
   LPSTR, ULONG, LONG, GUID) and TRUE/FALSE replaced by xfile_compat.h,
   a small header with no Windows dependency.
 - HRESULT return values replaced by a plain int error enum
   (xfile_compat.h's XFILE_OK/XFILE_ERR_*), mapping 1:1 onto the four
   DXFILEERR_* codes parsing.c actually returned.
 - TRACE/WARN/ERR/FIXME (Wine's debug-channel logging) compiled out -
   this engine has no equivalent trace level, and the checks they
   guarded are all still enforced (the codepath still fails, just
   silently instead of also logging).
 - HeapAlloc(GetProcessHeap(), ...)/HeapFree -> malloc/free.
 - stricmp -> a local case-insensitive compare (strcasecmp is POSIX-only;
   this keeps the vendored file portable the way the rest of engine/ is).
 - IDirectXFileImpl (the COM template registry) reduced to XFileTemplates,
   a plain struct holding just the xtemplates[]/nb_xtemplates arrays
   parsing.c actually reads through pdxf - every other field of the real
   COM object was for interfaces this vendoring does not implement.
 - check_buffer's first-grow memcpy(pdata, buf->pdata, buf->cur_pos_data)
   copies 0 bytes from a NULL buf->pdata on the very first call - benign
   in practice, but still undefined behaviour by the C standard, and
   UBSan (which this project runs its Debug builds under) flags it.
   Guarded with `if (buf->pdata)` before the copy; not present upstream.

The default D3DRM template set (Header/Mesh/MeshFace/MeshMaterialList/
Material/MeshTextureCoords/MeshNormals/MeshVertexColors/Frame/
FrameTransformMatrix/Vector/Coords2d - registered by Blitz3D's own
loader_x.cpp via D3DRM_XTEMPLATES before loading a file) is defined
separately in xfile_templates.h, as plain text of the public "x file
templates" - the same kind of format data this project already keeps for
Blitz3D's own command signatures, not third-party source, so it carries
no license header.

IMPORTANT caller contract: xfile_parse.c's parse_template_members_list
resolves a member whose type names another template (e.g. "array Vector
vertices[nVertices]") by searching xtemplates[1..nb_xtemplates) - index 0
is permanently reserved for the case-sensitive legacy type "indexColor"
(this was set up once, at IDirectXFile creation, in the original COM
object - see d3dxof.c's IDirectXFileImpl_Create). Call
xfile_reserve_index_color() on a freshly-zeroed XFileTemplates before
registering any real templates. Skipping this makes every "array
SomeTemplate member[n]" member fail to resolve its type, silently
breaking xfile_parse_templates() for any template set that references
another template (which the default D3DRM set does throughout - Mesh
references Vector and MeshFace, for instance).

Second contract point, also load-bearing for LoaderX: an xobject's
members[] array is transient scratch space for parse_object_members_list
at the CURRENT nesting level only. When a member's type is itself a
template (Mesh's "array Vector vertices[nVertices]"), parsing that
member recurses back into parse_object_members_list on the SAME xobject,
which overwrites members[]/nb_members with the nested template's own
layout. So members[] read back once the whole object has finished
parsing reflects whatever was parsed last (typically the final nested
member), not the top-level layout - reading pool[0].members[1] after
parsing a Mesh gets garbage, not "vertices".

This is not a vendoring bug: it is how the original COM object worked
too (IDirectXFileData::GetData(szMember) only made sense called right
after IDirectXFileData::GetNextObject returned that specific level, in
that specific streaming order), and it is exactly why loader_x.cpp never
calls GetData with a member name for a Mesh - it calls GetData(NULL),
which returns the object's raw bytes (root->pdata + pos_data) as one
blob, and walks them by hand following Mesh's own template layout
(int num_verts=*data++; ...). LoaderX must do the same: read the root
object's raw byte range and decode it according to the known template
layout, not by indexing back into members[] after the fact.

Verified with a standalone functional test parsing a real 3-vertex/
1-face Mesh block text .x this way, under ASan+UBSan.

Third contract point: a template that names another template as a
member's type (Mesh's "array Vector vertices[...]", MeshFaceWraps'
"array Boolean2d ...") can only reference a template ALREADY registered
- parse_template_members_list searches xtemplates[1..nb_xtemplates), and
nb_xtemplates is only incremented once the referencing template's own
parse_template() call returns. So the standard D3DRM template block
text passed to xfile_parse_templates() must declare templates in
dependency order (Vector/Coords2d/ColorRGB(A)/Boolean before
MeshFaceWraps/Mesh/MeshVertexColors/etc.), not any order a spec lists
them in - getting this wrong makes registration of a LATER template in
the same block fail, not just the member that has a bad forward
reference.

Fourth, a real-world note rather than a contract point: text .x objects
pack fields with no padding (parse_object_members_list writes a WORD
right after a DWORD, a pointer right after a WORD, etc.), so casting
pdata + some offset to DWORD*/float*/pointer-type and dereferencing it -
which is exactly what loader_x.cpp's parseMesh and every other original
Blitz3D .x/.b3d/.3ds reader does - hits genuinely misaligned addresses.
UBSan's alignment check (not the default build) flags this; it is
harmless on x86/x86-64 (unaligned access works, just slower) and was
already present in the original Wine parser and every Blitz3D-era
loader this project has ported so far, not something introduced by
vendoring. Confirmed reading a real production asset,
assets/tiny.x (a 1.5MB text .x, skinned mesh: 4432 vertices, 6841
faces, 48 frames, 35 SkinWeights blocks, one Material, one top-level
AnimationSet) end to end with the standard template set above.
